EXT. DRIVE-IN CONCESSION ROOF - NIGHT

Tar paper. A dead neon popcorn bucket. The big screen glows blue over a field of parked cars.

TAVITA SOLOMONA (17, linebacker shoulders, soft eyes) sits on the edge. ROZ KELLERMAN (17, sharp, camera strapped on) crouches near the vent. EAMON FLYNN (16, small, hands in his sleeves) arrives last through the hatch.

Eamon's boots on tin. Everyone freezes.

ROZ
You're late.

EAMON
My mom had me.

Tavita stands. Roz stands. They back away from the hatch. A flashlight beam sweeps the lot below.

TAVITA
Cops.

ROZ
Security.

TAVITA
It's the same thing.

ROZ
Get down!

All three drop flat. Tavita pulls Eamon by the collar. Roz's camera clacks on the tar. The beam slides past them, and passes. They breathe.

Then Eamon laughs. Tavita shoves him. Eamon shoves back. They tumble, wheezing, quiet as they can be.

ROZ
(whisper)
Stop it. Stop.

EAMON
Roz, you looked so scared.

ROZ
I was not.

TAVITA
You were crying.

ROZ
I was not!

EAMON
Tav, you cried too.

TAVITA
I cry at commercials. It doesn't count.

ROZ
You said you'd tell him tonight.

TAVITA
Tell who what?

ROZ
Eamon. About the move.

EAMON
What move?

TAVITA
It's nothing.

ROZ
It's Ohio.

EAMON
Ohio?

TAVITA
My dad's job. It's August. I didn't want to say it up here.

EAMON
You were gonna say it at the lake. You said that.

TAVITA
I know what I said.

ROZ
So say it.

TAVITA
I'm moving. I'm sorry. I'm sorry.

EAMON
Okay.

TAVITA
Okay?

EAMON
Okay. That's August. It's June.

ROZ
We have the whole summer.

EAMON
We have sixty-one nights. I counted.

Tavita wipes his face with his sleeve. Roz lifts her camera. The film flickers across all three of them, blue.

She takes the picture.
